data:   benchmarks/sse_madd_2026-09-05.tsv.

=================== 1. C'S SHAPE, WITH LINE NUMBERS ===================

  ASM_AVX2/pic_operators_intrin_avx2.c:802  svt_spatial_full_distortion_kernel_avx2
  ASM_AVX2/pic_operators_inline_avx2.h:111    spatial_full_distortion_kernel16_avx2_intrin
  ASM_AVX2/pic_operators_inline_avx2.h:103    distortion_avx2_intrin

and the kernel body (`pic_operators_inline_avx2.h:111-120`) is four
instructions per sixteen samples:

    in16 = _mm256_cvtepu8_epi16(input);   re16 = _mm256_cvtepu8_epi16(recon);
    diff = _mm256_sub_epi16(in16, re16);
    dist = _mm256_madd_epi16(diff, diff);   // d*d + d*d -> ONE i32 lane
    sum  = _mm256_add_epi32(sum, dist);     // i32 LANES, widened only at the end

Two further shapes worth naming, because the port copied neither. C's
`leftover == 8` arm (`pic_operators_intrin_avx2.c:831-847`) packs **two rows**
into one register — `_mm256_setr_m128i` of two `_mm_loadl_epi64` — and its
`leftover == 4` arm (`:815-830`) does the same with two `_mm_cvtsi32_si128`.
C never runs a narrow kernel on a narrow block; it makes the block wider.

=================== 2. WHAT THE PORT WAS DOING ===================

`crates/svtav1-dsp/src/variance.rs`'s x86 arm was the scalar double loop with a
`u64` accumulator, left to auto-vectorise:

    let diff = src[..] as i32 - ref_[..] as i32;
    sse += (diff * diff) as u64;

It DOES vectorise — but the `u64` accumulator forces every square into 64-bit
lanes, so the same work costs four times the vector registers. That one line
carried **21,363,780 Ir of `__arcane_sse_impl_v3`'s 27,915,788 self Ir** on
photo_cid p6 in `benchmarks/stall_attrib_2026-09-05.meta` §5, which is how this
chunk got its rank. It is the same class of finding as the CfL kernel's
i32-vs-i16 rounding (`benchmarks/cfl_simd_kernel_2026-09-05.meta`): the port had
the right algorithm at the wrong arithmetic width.

=================== 3. WHAT LANDED ===================

**Chunk 1 — C's `madd_epi16` shape as ONE `#[magetypes]` body**, on the
primitives archmage PR #96 adds (the PR that closes issue #89, which this
project filed): `u8x16::abs_diff` then `i16x8::madd_adjacent(self)`, summed in
i32 lanes and widened to u64 only at a drain. `|a-b|` squares to the same
integer as `a-b`, so `abs_diff` replaces C's two `cvtepu8` + `sub_epi16` and on
aarch64 is the single `vabdq_u8` the hand arm already used.

**Chunk 2 — C's row PACKING for widths 8 and 4**, in BOTH the generic body and
the NEON arm. `16 / width` rows go into one 16-byte vector, so a 4x4 block is
ONE fold instead of sixteen scalar iterations. The two 8- or 4-byte staging
copies stand in for x86's `setr_m128i`: magetypes has no vector-concatenate.
This is aimed at preset 2, where `sse` is 5.17 % of the frame's instructions
against 3.42 % at p6 — because at p2 most calls are transform units of 4 or 8
columns and a 16-lane kernel with a scalar tail degenerates to the scalar loop
on all of them.

The dev-only `[patch.crates-io]` pinning archmage/magetypes to PR #96's rev is
in `rust/Cargo.toml` with a comment saying so. **A fresh clone needs network
access to github.com/imazen/archmage**; when #96 merges and a release is cut,
delete the block and bump the registry requirement.

=================== 4. THE NUMBERS ===================

x86_64 frame Ir, r7900x callgrind, photo_cid 512² qp 40, EVERY ROW ident=Y:

  build        p6 Ir            port/C   p2 Ir             port/C
  nopatch      1,511,826,501    2.143    45,560,540,750    1.672
  patchonly    1,511,715,815    2.142    45,561,373,399    1.672
  cand (ch.1)  1,492,234,213    2.115    45,522,215,370    1.671
  cand2(ch.2)  1,482,868,791    2.102    44,686,279,137    1.640

  patchonly -> cand   p6 **-19,481,602 Ir = -1.289 %**;  p2 -39,158,029 = -0.086 %
  cand      -> cand2  p6 **-9,365,422 = -0.628 %**;   p2 **-835,936,233 = -1.836 %**

  nopatch -> cand2 (what a reader of `main` sees) p6 **-28,957,710 = -1.916 %**,
  p2 **-874,261,613 = -1.919 %**; port/C 2.143 -> 2.102 and 1.672 -> 1.640.

  **The LANDED tree was re-measured after the aarch64 revert, and reproduces
  it**: 1,482,868,495 at p6 (2.102) and 44,686,278,675 at p2 (1.640), ident=Y,
  same obu bytes — 296 and 462 Ir from the `cand2` rows, i.e. harness-path
  noise, not a code difference. (The revert touches only
  `#[cfg(target_arch = "aarch64")]` bodies, which an x86 build does not
  compile.) The census on that binary is 2,052, the same as `cand2`'s.

The kernel itself, SELF Ir at p6 (`callgrind_annotate`), over 30,484 calls:

  `svtav1_dsp::variance::__arcane_sse_impl_v3`
      patchonly  51,702,880 (3.42 % of frame)  ->  cand 32,080,468 (2.15 %)
      **-38.0 %**
  plus the outer `variance::sse` dispatch frame 792,584 -> 1,158,392, so the
  family is 52,495,464 -> 33,238,860, **-36.7 %**.
  C's `svt_spatial_full_distortion_kernel_avx2` is 7,701,126 over 59,952 calls
  (+ 2,757,792 in `svt_spatial_full_distortion_kernel_facade`).
  **The call counts are NOT comparable** — the port's `sse` and C's kernel are
  called at different granularities — so the ratio quoted is of TOTALS:
  port/C for the family 52.50/10.46 = **5.02x -> 3.18x**.

At p2 chunk 1 alone moves the same kernel 2,357,508,132 (5.17 %) ->
2,283,354,636 (5.02 %), and the family 2,436,872,092 -> 2,399,348,116,
**-1.54 %** — an order of magnitude less relief than p6 gets. **That gap IS the
measurement that motivated chunk 2**, and chunk 2 closes it:

  `variance::sse` family self Ir, base -> chunk 1 -> chunk 2, against C's
  (`svt_spatial_full_distortion_kernel_avx2` + `_facade`):

    cell  base            chunk 1         chunk 2         C             port/C
    p6    52,495,464      33,238,860      **19,234,004**  10,458,918    5.02x -> 3.18x -> **1.84x**
    p2    2,436,872,092   2,399,348,116   **1,124,658,211** 866,606,152 2.81x -> 2.77x -> **1.30x**

  i.e. **-63.4 % at p6 and -53.9 % at p2** from base, and the kernel goes from
  five times C's instructions to under twice at p6 and 1.3x at p2.

=================== 4b. WALL CLOCK, WITH ITS OWN SAME-BINARY CONTROL ===================

Run on r7900x only after the box was VERIFIABLY quiet — the script's own
`quiet.txt` records load 0.17 (1-minute) with the top process at 3.2 % CPU, and
a second stamp at the end (load 1.02, i.e. the runs themselves). 21 interleaved
randomised-order paired rounds per row, one untimed warmup per sample, NEITHER
SIDE NICED, every row `ident=Y`. `tools/perf_ab.sh`, `photo_cid` 512² raw yuv,
qp 40.

  CONTROL — `perf_encode.patchonly` against a BYTE COPY of itself:
    512 p2  ratio 0.9976   p25 0.9900   p75 1.0014
    512 p6  ratio 1.0009   p25 0.9906   p75 1.0059

  A/B — the same base against the LANDED binary:
    512 p2  ratio 0.9997   p25 0.9964   p75 1.0023
    512 p6  ratio 0.9911   p25 0.9880   p75 0.9938

**p6 is a real gain and is reported as 1.010x.** Corrected against its control
(0.9911 / 1.0009) the candidate is 0.9902, i.e. **1.010x**, and the A/B's WHOLE
interquartile span (0.9880-0.9938) sits below the control's median. **The spans
are NOT disjoint** — they share [0.9906, 0.9938] — so this is "medians
separated by 0.98 % with the candidate's box below the control's centre", not
"disjoint intervals", and it is written that way on purpose.

**p2 IS A NULL, and it disagrees with its own instruction count.** The A/B reads
0.9997 against a control of 0.9976; corrected that is 1.0021, i.e. no gain and
possibly a hair worse — against **-1.919 % of the frame's INSTRUCTIONS** at the
same preset. That is reported rather than explained: the honest statement is
that at p2 the removed instructions did not convert into time on this box, and
nothing here measures why. It is also the reason the control matters — without
it the p2 row (0.9997) would have read as a small win and the p6 row (0.9911)
as a slightly smaller one than it is.

=================== 5. aarch64: BOTH CHANGES MEASURED, BOTH REVERTED ===================

This is the result the campaign's rule ("measure before assuming a generic body
is as fast as the hand arm") exists for, and it went the way the rule warns —
TWICE. **The aarch64 arms in this commit are source-identical to `main`'s**
(asserted mechanically: the `sse_impl_neon` and `sse_impl_scalar` bodies were
diffed byte-for-byte against `7ec5b557`), so no aarch64 A/B of the LANDED tree
is needed or claimed. What follows is the record of the two things that were
tried and thrown away.

**5a. The generic `#[magetypes]` body is 1.45x-2.20x SLOWER than the hand NEON
arm.** magetypes lowers `i16x8::madd_adjacent` on aarch64 to
`vpaddq_s32(vmull_s16(lo, lo), vmull_high_s16(a, a))` — THREE instructions per
eight lanes — on top of two `vmovl_u8` widenings. The hand arm's
`vmull_u8` + `vpadalq_u16` pair squares and widens in TWO instructions with no
separate widening step at all. Eleven vector instructions per sixteen bytes
against five.

M4 Pro, `benches/kernel_tiers.rs`, THREE alternating runs of each build, mean
ns/call with the 95 % CI of the first run shown:

  case        hand arm (3 runs)           all-generic (3 runs)        ratio
  sse_16x16   21.3 / 22.1 / 20.2          30.9 / 30.0 / 31.0          1.45x
              [19.6-23.6]                 [29.6-31.7]
  sse_32x32   59.0 / 62.5 / 56.2          100.0 / 96.9 / 98.9         1.65x
  sse_64x64   186  / 200  / 189           420  / 413  / 423           2.20x
  sse_8x8     37.8 / 37.7 / 34.1          39.0 / 37.0 / 38.2          (overlap)
  sse_4x4     13.0 / 12.9 / 12.6          14.8 / 14.8 / 14.7          1.15x

**5b. C's row PACKING, staged through a `[u8; 16]`, is 1.32x-1.57x SLOWER here
— and it drags the UNTOUCHED `width >= 16` path down with it.** The wide loop's
source did not change at all between these two builds; only the two new
narrow-width branches were added around it, so the 16/32/64 rows are a
code-shape effect, not an arithmetic one. Same harness, same three-alternating-
runs protocol:

  case        hand arm (base)             + staged row packing        ratio
  sse_4x4     12.5 / 12.6 / 13.6 [12.4-12.7]                    20.0 / 20.5 / 20.1 [19.8-20.2]                    **1.57x**
  sse_8x8     37.3 / 36.7 / 37.4 [37.0-37.5]                    36.9 / 36.5 / 36.3 [36.7-37.2]                    0.98x (a null)
  sse_16x16   20.4 / 21.1 / 21.1 [19.7-21.1]                     27.9 / 27.4 / 28.0 [27.3-28.7]                     **1.33x**
  sse_32x32   58.2 / 59.1 / 58.9 [55.7-61.4]                     77.6 / 77.2 / 77.5 [75.8-79.8]                     **1.32x**
  sse_64x64   194 / 183 / 196 [187-202]                     253 / 253 / 256 [249-258]                     **1.33x**

**The open aarch64 item, priced and named:** the staging copies are what lost.
C does not stage — its `leftover == 8` arm is `_mm256_setr_m128i` of two
`_mm_loadl_epi64`, i.e. a REGISTER concatenate, and aarch64 spells that
`vcombine_u8(vld1_u8(row0), vld1_u8(row1))` with no memory round trip at all.
That form was NOT tried and is the next thing to bench here. On x86 the staged
form is a large Ir win and stays; magetypes has no vector-concatenate, so the
generic body has to stage.

So the generic route is taken on x86/wasm/scalar, where it replaces a
`u64`-accumulator scalar loop and wins, and NOT on aarch64, where it would
replace a kernel that is already better than what magetypes can emit.
**Reported on archmage#96.**

=================== 6. WHAT WAS FED BACK TO archmage#96 ===================

Three findings, all from this workspace:

1. **`i16xN::madd_adjacent` on NEON costs three instructions where the
   equivalent widening-accumulate costs two.** Numbers in §5.
2. **`u8xN::sum_abs_diff` fully REDUCES per call, so it cannot express a SAD
   accumulation loop.** The x86 backend is
   `_mm256_sad_epu8` then `_mm_add_epi64` + `_mm_srli_si128` + `_mm_cvtsi128_si64`
   (`magetypes/src/simd/impls/x86_v3.rs:4815`), and NEON is `vaddlvq_u8(vabdq_u8(..))`
   (`impls/arm_neon.rs:4527`) — a cross-lane reduction per 16 or 32 bytes. The
   port's `me_sad::block_sad_v3` and `block_sad_arm_v2` accumulate in
   `psadbw` / `vdotq_u32` lanes across the WHOLE block and reduce once, which
   the fused API cannot express. `me_sad` is therefore LEFT ALONE by this chunk,
   and the missing primitive is an ACCUMULATING form
   (`sad_accumulate(rhs, acc) -> u64xN`). **This is an instruction-sequence
   argument from the generated backends, NOT a wall-clock measurement.**
3. **There is no lane INTERLEAVE (`unpack_low` / `unpack_high` / `zip`) and no
   byte shuffle**, so C's two other pairwise shapes cannot be built at all:
   the Wiener vertical kernel's `_mm256_unpacklo_epi16` before `madd_epi16`
   (`ASM_AVX2/wiener_convolve_avx2.h:162`) and the horizontal kernel's
   `_mm256_shuffle_epi8` before `maddubs_epi16`. `madd_adjacent` is only half
   of what those need.

=================== 7. THE #95 QUESTION, ANSWERED ===================

The dispatch brief expected PR #95 ("removes redundant indexing assertions and
proves bounds-check elimination") to bear on the port's measured 2,042
`panic_bounds_check` sites, 72 of them in `quant::optimize_b::{closure#0}`.
**It does not, and the reason is structural:** #95 removes assertions from
magetypes' GENERATED indexing accessors, and at `7ec5b557` the port had **zero
magetypes generic-type call sites** — `grep -rn 'magetypes::' crates/` on the
base tree returns nothing but doc-comment prose in `residual.rs`, `me_sad.rs`
and `intra_pred.rs`. There was no site for #95 to act on.

Measured both ways:

  * the GOT-resolved census is **2,046** total in `perf_encode` for archmage
    0.9.28, for the #95+#96 stack, and for the stack plus this kernel, with the
    same per-function rows: `optimize_b::{closure#0}` 72,
    `inv_txfm::idct64` 69, `fwd_txfm::fdct64` 66, `idct32` 36, `fdct32` 34,
    `cost_coeffs_txb_inner::{closure#0}` 15. (This REPRODUCES
    `stall_attrib_2026-09-05`'s method and BOTH of its named per-function
    counts exactly. Its TOTAL was 2,042 against 2,046 here — four sites, and
    the two records are at different commits, so the totals are not expected to
    match to the unit; the per-function rows are the ones the argument rests
    on.)
  * the frame Ir for the patch alone is +-0.007 % with OPPOSITE SIGNS at the
    two presets (§4), i.e. inside the noise floor of nothing changing.

  **This commit ADDS six**, 2,046 -> 2,052: the row-packing staging loops
  (`sa[k * width..(k + 1) * width].copy_from_slice(..)`) index a `[u8; 16]` by a
  runtime product. They are off the 16-wide path entirely and the kernel is
  38 %/63 % cheaper anyway, so they are recorded rather than chased;
  `optimize_b::{closure#0}` is still exactly 72 and
  `cost_coeffs_txb_inner::{closure#0}` still 15.

So `optimize_b`'s 72 checks are plain Rust slice indexing in the port's own
code. Closing them is the documented fixed-size-array boundary
(`try_into::<&[T; N]>()` once), which is a `quant.rs` refactor and NOT something
an archmage bump can deliver. **A NOTE FOR WHOEVER TAKES IT: a naive
`objdump | grep panic_bounds_check` reads ZERO on this binary** — it is a PIE,
the calls are `call *0x..(%rip)` through a GOT slot, and the slot must be
resolved from the `R_X86_64_RELATIVE` addend before anything can be counted.
`stall_attrib` said so; this record confirms it by getting 0 the naive way and
2,046 the right way.

=================== 8. GATES ===================

**THE GATES BELOW ARE FROM THE MERGED TREE, AND THE MERGE MATTERS.** This
commit was rebased onto `373243b77` — the sibling lane's "port's FIRST AVX-512
tier" commit, which lands a `#[magetypes(define(i32x16), v4(cfg(avx512)), v3,
neon, wasm128, scalar)]` Wiener convolve and turns `svtav1-dsp`'s `avx512`
feature DEFAULT-ON. That commit was gated against **archmage 0.9.28**; this one
moves the whole workspace to the **#95+#96 git rev**, so its generic body's
codegen changes on BOTH ISAs, not just mine. The gate chain was therefore run
AGAIN, in full, on the rebased tree on both hosts — the numbers below are from
that second run, not from the pre-rebase one. (The pre-rebase chain was also
fully green; it is simply not the tree that ships.)

